#Where a peptide bond may be attacked: the reactive sites of the twenty amino
#acids' carbonyl carbons, read by peptidebond.sites.
#
#Format: <resname> <atomname> <antecedent> [[~|^]<plane>[:angle]]
#
#  C   the electrophile, the carbonyl carbon itself.
#  O   its antecedent, so the base direction is OPPOSITE C=O.
#  ^CA the plane reference, with '^' meaning the two directions LEAVE the
#      (C, O, CA) plane along its normal, one on each face.
#  :75 tilted 75 degrees off the base direction, which puts them 105 degrees
#      from the C=O axis.
#
#WHY 105 DEGREES. That is where the carbonyl's pi* lobe is, and a nucleophile
#that arrives anywhere else does not reach it -- Burgi & Dunitz (1974). The
#ribosome's own pre-attack state, 1VY4, measures 100.6 degrees between the
#A-site amine, the P-site carbonyl carbon and its oxygen, at 3.204 A.
#
#WITHOUT THIS FILE THE CONDITION IS NOT STRICTER, IT IS ABSENT. The angular
#factor is applied only when a site is valid (PeptideBond.cpp), so an
#electrophile with no entry here bonds on distance alone -- including to a
#nitrogen arriving edge-on, through the sp2 plane, where there is nothing to
#react with. A missing line makes the model bond MORE readily, not less.
#
#ATOM NAMES ARE PLAIN PDB ONES and need no renamed twin: the peptide bond term
#already carries peptidebond.naming for its own 'bond = C:N', and resolves
#these through it. One file serves a reduced topology and an unreduced one.
#
#BOTH ENDS OF THE REACTION ARE DECLARED HERE, and they have to be. This file,
#when given, is the ONLY table the peptide bond term reads -- it does not fall
#back to the .hbond for the half it is missing. An electrophile with no entry
#bonds on distance alone; a NUCLEOPHILE with no entry does the same, since the
#factor is applied per end. Declaring one end only would have made the model
#bond more readily than before, not less.
#
#  N   the nucleophile, the attacking nitrogen.
#  CA  its only heavy neighbour, so its lone pair points away from CA -- which
#      is the direction it attacks along.
#
#There is no plane column for the nitrogen, and no '-C' either: a nitrogen that
#can attack is a free terminus by construction (PeptideBondFormation::_isFree
#refuses one already bonded to another residue's carbonyl), so CA is all it has.
#A mid-chain amide nitrogen does need its previous residue's C to point its
#hydrogen, but that is the hydrogen bond's business and lives in the .hbond.
#
#Residue names are the standard three-letter codes. Add a non-standard residue
#by giving it the same columns; anything not named here simply has no site,
#which the run reports.

ALA  C  O  ^CA:75
ARG  C  O  ^CA:75
ASN  C  O  ^CA:75
ASP  C  O  ^CA:75
CYS  C  O  ^CA:75
GLN  C  O  ^CA:75
GLU  C  O  ^CA:75
GLY  C  O  ^CA:75
HIS  C  O  ^CA:75
ILE  C  O  ^CA:75
LEU  C  O  ^CA:75
LYS  C  O  ^CA:75
MET  C  O  ^CA:75
PHE  C  O  ^CA:75
PRO  C  O  ^CA:75
SER  C  O  ^CA:75
THR  C  O  ^CA:75
TRP  C  O  ^CA:75
TYR  C  O  ^CA:75
VAL  C  O  ^CA:75

ALA  N  CA
ARG  N  CA
ASN  N  CA
ASP  N  CA
CYS  N  CA
GLN  N  CA
GLU  N  CA
GLY  N  CA
HIS  N  CA
ILE  N  CA
LEU  N  CA
LYS  N  CA
MET  N  CA
PHE  N  CA
PRO  N  CA
SER  N  CA
THR  N  CA
TRP  N  CA
TYR  N  CA
VAL  N  CA
